output_text:
name: dotnet-testing-filesystem-testing-abstractions

Use for testing/refactoring file and directory operations using `System.IO.Abstractions`: `IFileSystem`, `MockFileSystem`, file reads/writes, paths, directory operations, and IO exception scenarios. Static `System.IO` operations are difficult to test due to disk speed, environment dependencies, side effects, race conditions, and hard-to-simulate errors.

Core interfaces: `IFileSystem` exposes `IFile File`, `IDirectory Directory`, `IFileInfo FileInfo`, `IDirectoryInfo DirectoryInfo`, `IPath Path`, and `IDriveInfo DriveInfo`.

Packages:
```xml
<PackageReference Include="System.IO.Abstractions" Version="21.*" />
<PackageReference Include="System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers" Version="21.*" />
```

Inject `IFileSystem` instead of calling static APIs; register `services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();` and `services.AddScoped<ConfigService>();`. In tests, use `MockFileSystem` with `MockFileData`/`MockDirectoryData` to create in-memory files and directories, then verify contents and final state with `mockFileSystem.File.Exists(...)`, `ReadAllTextAsync(...)`, and `mockFileSystem.Directory.Exists(...)`. Use a separate `MockFileSystem` per test.

For exceptions not well supported by `MockFileSystem`, use NSubstitute (`Substitute.For<IFileSystem>()`, `Substitute.For<IFile>()`) to configure calls such as `.Throws(new UnauthorizedAccessException("Access denied"))`. Test file-not-found, permissions, IO errors, line counts, file metadata, copy, and backup behavior.

Guidelines:
- Use `_fileSystem.Path.Combine("configs", "app.json")` for paths.
- Check `_fileSystem.File.Exists(filePath)` and create missing parent directories with `_fileSystem.Path.GetDirectoryName(filePath)` and `_fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(dir)`.
- Handle `UnauthorizedAccessException`, `IOException`, and `DirectoryNotFoundException`.
- Use independent MockFileSystem instances for tests.

1. **Hardcode path separators**:
// ❌ Don't do this
var path = "configs\\app.json";  // Windows only
var path = "configs/app.json";   // Unix only
// ✅ Should do this
var path = _fileSystem.Path.Combine("configs", "app.json");
2. **Use real file system in unit tests**:
// ❌ This is not a unit test
var realFs = new FileSystem();
// ✅ Unit tests should use MockFileSystem
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem();
3. **Ignore exception handling**:
// ❌ Don't assume file always exists
var content = _fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);
// ✅ Add existence check and exception handling
if (_fileSystem.File.Exists(path))
try { return _fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path); }
catch (IOException) { return defaultValue; }
Only create files necessary for testing
Avoid simulating oversized files in tests

For large-file logic, use moderately sized test data, e.g. 1,000 lines with `Enumerable.Range(1, 1000)` and `mockFileSystem.AddFile("test.txt", new MockFileData(testContent));`.

See `templates/configmanager-service.cs` for config load/save, JSON serialization, directory creation, and backups. See `templates/filemanager-service.cs` for file copy/backup, metadata, and error handling.

Related skills: `nsubstitute-mocking`, `unit-test-fundamentals`.
Source: kevintsengtw/dotnet-testing-agent-skills (MIT), ported into dotnet-agent-harness. References: https://ithelp.ithome.com.tw/articles/10375981; https://github.com/kevintsengtw/30Days_in_Testing_Samples/tree/main/day17; https://github.com/TestableIO/System.IO.Abstractions; https://www.nuget.org/packages/System.IO.Abstractions/; https://www.nuget.org/packages/System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers/.
